    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="4" build="5">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.4b5</English>
                <French>v15.4b5</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage. This is partially due to the nature of log homes. I did note less air leakage in the upstairs area of your home which has been renovated.



I noted a significant amount of air leakage around your windows and doors. 



Since it was not working at the time of my site visit, I recommend you replace your range hood fan with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



Due to technical problems with your file, default air leakage values were used. However, because the circumstances were beyond your control, you are eligible for the air sealing rebate should you choose to do any of this work. The minimum decrease in air leakage to be eligible for a rebate through AEA is 10%. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 19% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $75&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

I have not modelled adding insulation to the kitchen and living room of your home, or to the upstairs area. The original cabin has 2x4 framing with R12 insulation, and may be missing some insulation (in particular, where the original door may be located).



I have modelled increasing the insulation in this room by R5, to approximately R22.  



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R5 of insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $60&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. Because this only represents approximately 15% of your exterior wall area, you would not qualify for any rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The accessible part of the attic of your house has 4 inches (R12) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled increasing the insulation in your attic to approximately R50. 



In order to add insulation to your attic, you will also need to add soffits to the edge of your attic space in order to protect the insulation. 



Since you have more than one type of ceiling in your home, the rebate is pro-rated based on the ceiling area that is insulated. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing your attic insulation from R12 to R50, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $350&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;13.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$501 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



I have not modelled the energy savings associated with replacing your doors, but if you replace them I look for ENERGY STAR® qualified doors rated for climate zone 3



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $270&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an older 189 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. 



If you decide to continue to use electricity to heat your hot water, I recommend adding an R5 insulation blanket to your hot water tank. 



Another option is to replace your electric hot water tank with a propane fired instantaneous hot water heater, with an efficiency factor of 0.92 or greater. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an R5 insulation blanket to your electric hot water heater, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $85&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for this measure from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



By replacing your electric hot water heater, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $840&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and be eligible for a rebate of &lt;b&gt;$1,250&lt;/b&gt; through the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace is relatively new, and is rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Another option is to switch from oil to propane, and install a direct vent, condensing propane fired furnace with an efficiency of 0.95 or higher. 



Using your pellet stove for 50% of the heating load on your main floor will save you approximately $210 per year in energy costs. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $215&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates for furnaces of this efficiency from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,260&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;30.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Smith&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.243 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.528 per litre

Wood - $262.50 per cord 

Pellets - $7.30 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



&lt;font color="lightseagreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
